---
import FirstRunTable from "../FirstRunTable.astro";
import Pipeline from "./Pipeline.astro";
import Voicemail from "./Voicemail.astro";
import { reported } from "../../lib/first-run";
import { bounds, story } from "../../lib/coverage";
import { noScore, pipeline, rileyAsk, spell } from "../../lib/cases";

const kickPlan = [
  `Download the schedule page from each of the ${spell(pipeline.asked)} school sites, and the box scores Alder Ridge's schedule links.`,
  "Parse each results table into rows: date, opponent, score.",
  "If a page fails to load, retry up to three times, then log a warning with the school's name and continue so the run finishes.",
  "Read each match once, from the home team's page, so none counts twice.",
  "Compute played, goal difference and points, then sort and write the table.",
  "Print a summary: pages fetched, matches found, errors.",
];

const openings = [
  { gap: "How many matches to expect", kick: "Report the number found.", then: `${story.counted}, with nothing to compare it against.`, href: "#case-dunmore", where: "Case 1" },
  { gap: "Which page to read each match from", kick: "The home team's page, once, so no match counts twice.", then: `One lost page wiped out ${spell(bounds.unseen)} matches. Case 2 shows what else it skewed.`, href: "#case-hartwell", where: "Case 2" },
  { gap: "What to do with a final that has no score", kick: "Unspecified. Any row without a score was quietly dropped.", then: `${noScore.home} and ${noScore.away} are each one match short.`, href: "#case-cedar-grove", where: "Case 3" },
  { gap: "What to do when a site doesn't answer", kick: "Retry three times, log a warning, and keep going.", then: "The run finished without Dunmore.", href: "#decision", where: "The decision" },
  { gap: "What the final summary should report", kick: "Pages fetched, matches found, and errors.", then: "“All eight school sites, no errors.”", href: "#rewind", where: "Rewind" },
];

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const film = (name: string) => `${base}film/${name}`;

const filmScript = [
  "You want a league table for eight schools. The results are spread across eight websites, on pages made for people, not programs.",
  "To collect them, you write a scraper: a program that downloads those pages and pulls rows out of them. The simplest one is a single loop.",
  "For each site: download the page, find the results table, read each row, and add it to the standings. On a good day, it works. Then, one at a time, five ordinary things go wrong.",
  "First: one site doesn't answer. The loop waits, gives up, and crashes, taking the finished sites down with it.",
  "So downloading becomes its own stage: scrape. It works down a list of addresses, checks whether each site answered, waits, and tries again. A silent site is now scrape's to deal with, instead of a crash. What it should do about one is a real decision. Hold on to that.",
  "Second: a month later, you find a bug. Your code had home and away scores backwards. You fix it and go back for the pages, but the sites have changed, and some of last month's pages are gone.",
  "That brings in store. Save every page exactly as it arrived, before any of your code reads it. Now a bug is cheap. Fix the code and run it again on the saved pages, without asking the web for anything.",
  "Third, and quieter: a site is redesigned, and a ticket table now sits above the results. The loop reads the first table it finds, the wrong one, and nothing complains.",
  "Reading the page becomes its own stage: parse. It finds the schedule by its label, not its position, and hands on rows in one fixed shape: date, opponent, result.",
  "Fourth: put the rows together. A match between A and B is listed on A's page, and again on B's. Add up every row, and every match counts twice.",
  "That forces build, where rows become matches. It decides which copy of each match to keep, and only then works out the standings. Which copy it keeps is another quiet choice. Hold on to that one, too.",
  "Fifth: the run ends and hands you a table. Did every site answer? How many rows came in? Were there errors? The table can't say.",
  "That is report's job. It writes down what the run did: the pages it fetched, the rows it found, and the errors it hit.",
  "Line the five stages up, and one loop has become a pipeline. Scrape hands pages to store. Store hands saved pages to parse. Parse hands rows to build. Build makes the table, and hands its counts to report. Each stage can be checked on its own, and run again without starting over.",
  "Now read that report again. Eight pages fetched, one hundred and four rows, no errors. Every line is about what the run did. Not one says what the run should have found. That gap is what this module is about.",
];

const learnPrompt =`I'm a data science student learning how data is collected from websites. Teach me what a web scraper is and how it becomes a data pipeline. Use one running example throughout: collecting a season of match results from eight school athletics sites to build a league table.

1. Walk me through the parts a scraper needs: a list of what to fetch, fetching (requests, status codes, retries, rate limits, robots.txt), storing the raw pages before parsing them, parsing HTML tables into rows, building the output, and reporting on the run. For each part, tell me why it exists and what goes wrong without it.
2. Show how those parts chain into a pipeline: what each stage takes in and what it hands to the next.
3. For each stage, name one way it can lose data without raising an error.
4. Sketch the stages as separate Python functions, working on a saved HTML string, not a live site.
5. End with three questions that check my understanding. Wait for my answers before you give yours.`;

const choices = [
  ["stop", "Stop the run"],
  ["skip", "Skip it and carry on"],
  ["fill", "Fill in 0-0"],
  ["record", "Record the gap"],
];
---

<section id="setup">
  <h2>Setup: one prompt, one Friday</h2>
  <p>Let's slow Friday down and walk through what actually went wrong behind the scenes.</p>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>1</span>The prompt</h3>
  <div class="cv-user-prompt" role="region" aria-label="Riley's prompt to Kick">
    <div class="cv-chat-bar">
      <span class="cv-chat-badge">Prompt</span>
      <span class="cv-chat-meta">Riley → Kick</span>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-chat-text">{rileyAsk}</p>
  </div>
  <p>It names the season, the sport, the sources, and the output. At a glance, it sounds complete.</p>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>2</span>What Kick built</h3>
  <p>Kick built a web scraper, a program that downloads pages meant for people and extracts structured rows from them. This 4-minute video builds one from a single loop and shows five ordinary ways it breaks.</p>
  <figure class="cv-film">
    <video controls playsinline preload="metadata" poster={film("one-loop-to-a-pipeline.jpg")} aria-label="One Loop to a Pipeline, an animated explainer under four minutes">
      <source src={film("one-loop-to-a-pipeline.mp4")} type="video/mp4" />
      <track kind="captions" src={film("one-loop-to-a-pipeline.vtt")} srclang="en" label="English" default />
      <track kind="chapters" src={film("one-loop-to-a-pipeline-chapters.vtt")} srclang="en" label="Chapters" />
    </video>
    <figcaption>One Loop to a Pipeline. Schools are lettered and the counts are illustrative.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <details class="cv-more">
    <summary>Read the video narration</summary>
    {filmScript.map((x) => <p>{x}</p>)}
  </details>
  <p>Kick's pipeline followed those same five stages. Here is what happened at each step on Friday.</p>
  <Pipeline />
  <details class="cv-more">
    <summary>Kick's plan, in its own words</summary>
    <ol class="cv-plan">{kickPlan.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ol>
  </details>
  <p>Whenever you check a data pipeline, there are three main questions to ask:</p>
  <ul class="cv-three">
    <li class="is-here"><b>Is everything here?</b><span><strong>Completeness</strong>, measured as coverage. This module.</span></li>
    <li><b>Is each value in a usable form?</b><span>Cleansing. <a href="../04-parsing/">Modules 4</a> and <a href="../05-identity/">5</a>.</span></li>
    <li><b>Does each record obey the rules?</b><span>Validation. <a href="../06-contracts/">Modules 6</a> and <a href="../07-gates/">7</a>.</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p>All {story.counted} of Kick's matches are real and accurately parsed, so both cleansing and validation would pass this table. A missing record gives those checks nothing to fail.</p>
  <details class="cv-learn-box">
    <summary>New to web scrapers? Open an AI study prompt</summary>
    <div class="cv-prompt cv-learn">
      <p class="cv-prompt-note">Paste this into any AI assistant for a quick primer on scrapers and pipelines, then come back for the cases.</p>
      <p data-copy-text tabindex="0">{learnPrompt}</p>
      <button type="button" data-copy>Copy prompt</button>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>3</span>What the prompt left open</h3>
  <p>Riley's one-sentence prompt left five important details unspecified. Kick filled each one with a reasonable default and never mentioned any of them. Click each item to see what happened.</p>
  <div class="cv-open">
    {openings.map((o) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{o.gap}</summary>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Kick chose</dt><dd>{o.kick}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Result</dt><dd>{o.then}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Covered in</dt><dd data-rail-hint={o.href.slice(1)}>{o.where}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </details>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>4</span>What Riley saw</h3>
  <p>When Kick finished, this was everything on Riley's screen.</p>
  <div class="cv-agent-reply" role="region" aria-label="Kick's completion summary">
    <div class="cv-chat-bar">
      <span class="cv-agent-badge">Kick · Coding agent</span>
      <span class="cv-status-ok">Done</span>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-agent-text">Done. All eight school sites, {story.counted} conference matches, no errors. Ready to publish.</p>
  </div>
  <FirstRunTable rows={reported} caption="The table Kick delivered" />
  <div class="cv-commit" data-commit>
    <label class="cv-write" for="cv-looks">Take a look at the table above. Does anything look wrong to you? <span class="cv-ungraded">(Not graded)</span></label>
    <textarea id="cv-looks" rows="2" placeholder="Jot down a quick observation..." data-save="looks-wrong"></textarea>
    <div class="cv-actions">
      <button type="button" data-commit-btn disabled>Save note and continue</button>
      <button type="button" class="cv-link-btn" data-commit-skip>Skip and show takeaway</button>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once" data-needs-note>Write a short sentence to unlock, or skip ahead.</p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <p>Eight rows, neatly sorted, plausible numbers, no blanks. Dunmore sitting at {spell(bounds.played)} games played only looks suspicious if you already know how long the season is.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>5</span>How the problem surfaced</h3>
  <p>Riley published the table on Friday afternoon. Within an hour, the coaches caught what Riley had no way to see.</p>
  <div class="cv-calls">
    <Voicemail who="dolan" caller="Coach Dolan, Hartwell" line="We went into Dunmore October fifteenth and put seven on 'em. Seven-nothing. You've got us fourth." />
    <Voicemail who="dunmore" caller="Dunmore athletics" line="You've got our men at six conference games. We've played thirteen." />
    <Voicemail who="cedar" caller="Cedar Grove men's soccer" note="from the bus" line="September twenty-first, us and St. Lambert. That game happened. It's not in the standings." />
  </div>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>6</span>The core problem</h3>
  <p class="cv-lede">Kick's summary reported on its code, not on the data. Every row in the table is clean and valid, yet key matches are missing.</p>
  <div class="cv-say cv-yours" data-first hidden>
    <p class="cv-who">Your question from Publish Day</p>
    <p data-first-text></p>
  </div>
  <fieldset class="cv-choice" data-gate>
    <legend>Dunmore's site doesn't answer. What should Kick do?</legend>
    {choices.map(([value, label]) => (
      <label><input type="radio" name="cv-decision" value={value} data-save="decision" /><span>{label}</span></label>
    ))}
  </fieldset>
  <p class="cv-once">Not graded. We'll revisit your choice after the three cases.</p>
  <p class="cv-can">Next, we'll introduce the core idea behind all three voicemails and walk through one case per call. By the end of this module, you'll be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define how many items a collection should find before it runs, using an outside source</li>
    <li>trace a missing source to the block of records it drops, and calculate bounds on how much the numbers could shift</li>
    <li>distinguish <em>empty</em>, <em>unknown</em>, and <em>never asked</em>, and know what each lets you claim</li>
    <li>decide how a collector should handle a silent source, and what makes a run successful</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="cv-next">Up next: the concept behind all three calls, coverage.</p>
</section>
